ile retrieving more documents increases the chance of including relevant information (recall), the ranking quality plateaus, introducing noise. Our findings show that:\n- DRAG performance peaks around 100-500 documents (Figure 5b), aligning with NDCG/MRR saturation, then plateaus or declines\n- IterDRAG mitigates this by using simpler sub-queries that retrieve more focused results—Table 5 shows IterDRAG achieves 30.5% average improvement in NDCG and 39.9% in MRR\n\nThis is not a flaw but rather evidence that IterDRAG's query decomposition strategy is effective at managing noise. The paper already provides this analysis in Appendix B and Section A. Future work on re-ranking (acknowledged in Section A) could further improve this, but the current strategies already substantially address the issue for complex queries.\n\n**On 1.2k dataset sampling:** We followed the practice of Wu et al. (2024) and Gutiérrez et al. (2024) to manage computational costs when conducting extensive hyperparameter searches (k ∈ {0, 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 50, 100, 200, 500, 1000}, m ∈ {2^0, ..., 2^8}, n up to 5) across four datasets. This represents 1,200 parameter configurations per dataset. Critically, we observe consistent patterns across all four datasets despite this sampling, suggesting robustness. The scaling laws (Figure 4, Figure 11) show nearly identical trends across datasets, which would not be expected if sampling introduced substantial bias. Additionally, our model generalizes to unseen domains with 96.6% oracle alignment (Table 3), demonstrating the findings are not artifacts of sampling bias.\n\n**On computation allocation model theoretical justification:** The inverse sigmoid parametrization in Equation 2 was chosen empirically to capture the sub-linearity observed at extreme context lengths (>1M). We chose this form because: (1) performance follows log-linear behavior for moderate lengths but saturates beyond 1M tokens, (2) sigmoid-like saturation curves are theoretically motivated