gression are presented as fixed without sensitivity analysis. Table 6's ablations are component-level (with/without SR, with/without DND), not hyperparameter-level.\n\nFor revision, we will conduct systematic sensitivity studies:\n\n- **α ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2}**: Tests the strength of label smoothing in confidence-based refinement.\n- **K ∈ {5%, 10%, 15%, 20%}**: Tests the noise detection percentile threshold.\n- **Warmup threshold ∈ {10%, 20%, 30%}**: Tests when denoising begins.\n- **λ progression**: Compare constant λ vs. progressive reduction vs. other schedules.\n\nWe expect results to show robustness around the chosen values (hyperparameter choices matter but are not critical), which strengthens generalizability claims. These experiments are straightforward and should appear in revision.\n\n**On task-specific degradation (Math/Coding):**\n\nYou note that while Appendix C Figure 5a shows near-zero gain in Coding and degradation in Math, the paper simply references this as \"commonly observed\" without investigating SPA-specific causes. This is a fair critique—acknowledging the phenomenon without analysis leaves it unclear whether it reflects fundamental limitations of SPA or data/hyperparameter issues.\n\nFor revision, we propose investigating:\n\n1. **Seed data analysis**: Compute what fraction of the 2K seed data covers Math/Coding tasks. If underrepresented, this explains poor performance—preference judgment in these domains is unreliable when not well-represented in seed data.\n\n2. **Domain-specific validation**: Use a small hand-curated validation set to measure preference judgment accuracy separately for Math, Coding, Writing, etc. If direct judgment has domain-specific biases, this quantifies them.\n\n3. **Domain-adaptive hyperparameters**: Test whether increasing K (identifying more uncertain samples) for technical tasks improves performance. This would suggest SPA benefits from domain-specific tuning.\n\nThese analyses do require new experiments but are feasible within revision scope and would either explain the degradation or point to genuine limitations of the current approach.\n\n**Summary of revision strategy:**\n\nWe appreciate your clear distinction between:\n- **Clarifications** (Eq. 12 intuition, progressive λ justification): Addressable via exposition, no new experiments needed.\n- **Integration issues** (length control in main pipeline): Requires rerunning experiments with modified preference judgment, but no fundamental algorithm change.\n- **Analysis gaps** (distribution shift characterization, hyperparameter sensitivity, task-specific investigation): Requires new experiments but is feasible and will strengthen the paper.\n\nNone of these revisions require abandoning the core contribution (direct preference judgment achieving 30× data efficiency improvement). Rather, they strengthen the paper's theoretical grounding, experimental rigor, and practical clarity. We are committed to addressing all identified concerns in the revision, and we believe the resulting paper will meet the standards of a top-tier venue."
