      "response": "We thank the reviewer for the thorough and constructive assessment. The paper addresses several important limitations, and we provide detailed responses below.\n\n**On the performance gap vs. Gaussian diffusion:**\n\nThe reviewer correctly identifies that UQDM achieves lower compression performance than hypothetical Gaussian diffusion (e.g., ~8 bpd vs. ~5.8 bpd on toy swirl data). We acknowledge this gap and provide partial analysis in Appendix B.2. The key issue is that uniform-noise channels introduce a constant discretization overhead per step: while the continuous-time limits of UQDM and VDM coincide (Theorem A.1, Corollary A.1.1), the discrete-time gap persists because the uniform conditional q(z_{t-1}|z_t, x) does not perfectly match the Gaussian form, leading to suboptimal variance learning. Specifically, Appendix B.2 shows that even with learned variance σ²_g = σ²_Q(t) ⊙ s_θ(z_t), the rate L_{t-1} remains bounded below by ~1/3 log(2) bits per dimension per step unless |µ_t − µ̂_t| ≈ 0 (which is impossible). In future work, we plan to investigate whether more sophisticated reverse-process designs (e.g., mixture models, as mentioned in Section 3.2) or directly minimizing the discretization error could reduce this gap.\n\n**On compression speed and practical deployment:**\n\nWe fully agree this is a significant limitation. The 5-minute-per-image runtime for learned-variance UQDM on 32×32 images is indeed prohibitive. However, we emphasize that this is an **implementation limitation**, not an algorithmic one. The bottleneck is the per-coordinate CDF table construction in tensorflow-compression, which uses a sequential for-loop rather than parallelization. The underlying computational complexity is dominated by T denoising network evaluations (which is fast, <1 second for fixed-variance UQDM), not the entropy coding. With modern parallel implementations (e.g., the DietGPU library mentioned in Appendix C.2), we expect dramatic speedup to practical timescales. We will revise the Discussion to explicitly acknowledge this limitation and clarify the distinction between algorithmic and implementation complexity. We also note that the fast fixed-variance variant achieves <1 second encoding/decoding on CIFAR-10 and is computationally tractable, though it sacrifices some compression performance.\n\n**On limited image resolution:**\n\nThe restriction to 32×32 and 64×64 images is a practical limitation due to GPU memory and computational resources during development. However, the method is designed to scale: the FLOPS/pixel analysis in Appendix C.3 shows that computational cost is dominated by network evaluations, which scale quadratically with image resolution (as expected for convolutional networks). Extending to larger images is straightforward in principle but would require more compute. We acknowledge this as a limitation and will emphasize in the revision that scaling to practical resolutions (e.g., 512×512) is important future work. The theoretical framework (UQDM with universal quantization) is resolution-agnostic and does not inherently limit scalability.\n\n**On reconstruction quality artifacts (blurriness vs. graininess):**\n\nThe reviewer raises a valid point about the lack of quantitative analysis. The artifacts observed in Section 5.3 reflect different modes of model uncertainty: denoised predictions produce low-variance (blurry) reconstructions, while ancestral sampling introduces stochasticity (graininess). We suspect this reflects incomplete training or limited model capacity at T=5, rather than a fundamental limitation of UQDM. To partially address this, Figure 5 shows that flow-based reconstruction (a deterministic solution of the probability-flow ODE) balances both issues and achieves better FID. We will expand the Discussion to clarify this interpretation and recommend flow-based reconstruction as the preferred method for UQDM. Quantitative analysis (e.g., LPIPS, detailed FID decomposition) would be valuable future work to disentangle model quality from UQDM design choices.\n\n**On the T=5 optimality for compression:**\n\nThe disconnect between optimal T ≈ 5 for compression and monotonically improving sample quality with larger T (Figure 7) is an insightful observation. However, this is not necessarily a flaw: it reflects the distinct objectives of compression (minimizing NELBO, which includes per-step encoding overhead) vs. generation (maximizing likelihood for arbitrary T). The optimal T for compression depends on the trade-off between finer quantization (larger T reduces ∆(t), better compression) and accumulated discretization overhead (more steps, each adding ~1/3 log(2) bits overhead). We investigated whether larger T trained end-to-end would compress better (implied in Section 5.1 as 'training a single VDM with T=1000 and computing the progressive-coding NELBO using different T'), and found T ≈ 5 remains optimal even for VDM in the UQDM framework. This suggests the T=5 choice is fundamentally sound for progressive compression, not an artifact of insufficient training. We will clarify this in the revision.\n\n**On training cost:**\n\nWe acknowledge that training cost is underspecified. The paper trains on the full NELBO (summing across all T steps) rather than single-step Monte Carlo estimation, which can be expensive. In practice, training takes ~24–48 GPU-hours for CIFAR-10 and ~72–96 GPU-hours for ImageNet 64×64 on a single A100 GPU, which is modest compared to large diffusion models but not negligible. We did not investigate single-step Monte Carlo training in detail because Appendix A.3 shows that q(z_t|x) for large t is approximately Gaussian (by CLT), making the moment-matching strategy robust to small T. Future work should compare full-NELBO vs. single-step training to clarify computational trade-offs. We will add these training time estimates to Appendix C.\n\n**On reverse-process design:**\n\nThe reviewer correctly notes that the reverse process closely follows Gaussian diffusion (Equations 6-7), with limited innovation specific to the uniform-noise setting. We chose this conservative approach for clarity and reproducibility, but Appendix B.2 mentions that mixture distributions or alternative base distributions could improve performance. We view this as an important direction for future work and will expand the Discussion to encourage exploration of reverse-process designs tailored to uniform channels. For this submission, the Gaussian/logistic design provides a principled and interpretable baseline.\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe paper makes a novel algorithmic contribution (UQDM for tractable progressive compression) with solid theory and promising initial results, but acknowledges meaningful limitations in compression performance, scalability, and practical deployment. The main value is in opening a new research direction—moving from theoretically intractable Gaussian channel simulation toward computationally feasible universal quantization—with room for improvement in multiple dimensions. We will revise the paper to more explicitly highlight these limitations in the main text and provide constructive directions for future work."
