rstates this contribution by claiming to provide *human-interpretable* explanations without validating this claim, by promising a *user-centric framework* that is not implemented, and by claiming *generalizability* while relying on manual extensions.\n\nThe work would benefit substantially from:\n1. Repositioning the contribution as an application paper that combines orbits with weight decomposition to provide structure-aware explanations, without overstatement about human interpretability.\n2. Conducting user studies to either validate or retire the interpretability claim.\n3. Either extending to user-defined graphlets or clearly acknowledging the 2-5 node constraint upfront.\n4. Providing theoretical analysis of the approximation quality.\n5. Expanding real-world validation with domain expert assessment.\n\n**Why We Cannot Simply Accept the Reviewer's Proposed Revisions in Rebuttal:**\n\nThe reviewer's proposed future work (user studies, theory, expert validation, feature development) is substantial and fundamentally changes the paper. We cannot claim these revisions are 'already in the paper' or will be added without major new effort. To do so would be to extend the pattern of overstating what the submission contains.\n\n**Concluding Assessment:**\n\nWe believe the reviewer's evaluation is fair and well-justified. The gaps they identify are real and significant. The paper, as submitted, makes contributions that are genuine but more modest than claimed. For a top-tier venue that expects rigorous validation of core claims and sound theoretical foundations, the work requires substantial additional effort. We respect the reviewer's clear articulation of what is missing and will use this feedback to guide a major revision that addresses these gaps properly.\n"
