ant and may yield new insights.\"\n\nHowever, we believe the current evidence is reasonably strong for several reasons: (1) We demonstrate collapse and supercollapse across three distinct domains—next-token prediction on images (CIFAR-5M), chess moves (Lichess), and synthetic regression—and across different architectures (transformers, MLPs). (2) Our scaling ladder covers more than an order of magnitude in parameter count (10M to 80M), which is non-trivial. (3) Recent work (Wortsman et al. 2023) has shown that instability phenomena observed at modest scales do manifest at larger scales. (4) Most importantly, we are studying a phenomenon rooted in compute-optimal scaling and power-law behavior, both of which are well-established at larger scales (Kaplan et al. 2020, Hoffmann et al. 2022). The universality of power-law scaling laws across scales suggests our scaling-invariant findings should transfer.\n\nWe agree that validation at larger scales (e.g., 1B parameters) would significantly strengthen the claims and would be valuable future work.\n\n**On Tr(Σ) scaling in cross-entropy:** The reviewer identifies our deferred explanation for why Tr(Σ) ∝ L despite irreducible loss in cross-entropy. This is an honest gap we acknowledge. In linear regression with label noise, the gradient covariance scales with total loss L rather than reducible loss, which should theoretically hold here too. Our empirical observation (Figure 7) confirms this scaling holds despite irreducible loss. Rather than speculate, we deferred this to future work. However, the practical success of our model (Figures 6, 7 showing strong quantitative agreement) suggests the phenomenon is real and captures genuine dynamics, even if the theoretical explanation requires more careful treatment.\n\n**On the δΣ term:** The reviewer notes it is 'puzzling' that excluding the δΣ term sometimes improves fits. Section 3.2.2 shows this term is typically 3-10x smaller than the δη term during decay, which we report. For