      "response": "Thank you for the thorough and constructive review. We appreciate the balanced assessment and detailed feedback. We address the key concerns below:\n\n**On Limited Scope and Applicability:**\nWe agree that RLEF's applicability is constrained to domains with automatic execution and deterministic correctness signals. This is inherent to the method and acknowledged in Section 5. However, we note that competitive programming, while specific, is a valuable testbed for agent capabilities—it requires synthesis, debugging, algorithmic reasoning, and iterative refinement. We also note our brief mention of automatic test generation (Watson et al., 2020; Jain et al., 2024a) as a complementary approach for domains lacking pre-existing test cases. Extending RLEF to such domains is interesting future work, as is the combination with weaker correctness signals (static analysis, type checking) that we do not explore here.\n\n**On Generalization and Single-Turn Performance Degradation:**\nYour observation about the 8B model's single-turn degradation (11.8 → 9.7) is valid. Unlike the 70B model, which improves in single-turn (26.2 → 30.1), the 8B model shows this asymmetry. We interpret this as follows: (1) The 8B model, having weaker initial capabilities, may specialize more to the multi-turn regime during RL training, whereas the 70B model's stronger priors allow it to improve both single and multi-turn; (2) The KL penalty may constrain the 8B model's explorations more severely. Notably, Table 4 shows single-turn RL training on the 8B model itself degrades performance (10.2 vs 8.9 baseline), suggesting the 8B model's representation may be less amenable to RL-based optimization in single-turn scenarios compared to the 70B. We did not include this analysis in the main paper but acknowledge it as an important limitation worth discussing. The 70B model's more robust improvements across settings make it the more reliable choice.\n\n**On Turn Limit Generalization (Figure 4b):**\nThe observation that best 10@k performance shifts from 3 (training regime) to 5 turns is expected: at larger sample budgets (10@k), distributing samples across more turns provides increased opportunity for recovery from errors, even if single attempts are less reliable. This is consistent with the pass@10 results in Figure 4a, where true feedback shows increasing pass rates with turn limit but random feedback does not. We interpret this as the model learning to generate diverse candidate solutions, not specialization per se. That said, your suggestion to explore training with different turn limits would be valuable—if training on 5 turns yields better 5-turn performance at inference without sacrificing 3-turn performance, that would strengthen generalization claims. This is a reasonable ablation we did not conduct.\n\n**On Hyperparameter Sensitivity:**\nYou correctly note that we provide limited ablation on β=0.05 and the -0.2 invalid code penalty. While our early experiments motivated these choices, systematic sensitivity analysis would strengthen robustness claims. We acknowledge this as a limitation. A brief ablation on β (e.g., {0.01, 0.05, 0.1}) or the invalid code penalty for at least the 8B model would be informative. We did not include this in the submission but recognize it as a reasonable addition for revision.\n\n**On Sample Efficiency Claims:**\nWe appreciate the precision point. The abstract's claim of \"order of magnitude\" improvement is somewhat imprecise when comparing different n@k metrics. A fairer framing: our 70B model achieves 40.1 solve rate (1@3) with 3 samples per problem, substantially outperforming AlphaCodium's 29 solve rate (5@100, i.e., 500 samples per problem at best). The n@k metric differs, but we use it consistently with prior work (Li et al., 2022). Footnote 1 acknowledges that sample budget may not be fully utilized due to early stopping when public tests pass, but the core claim—fewer model responses needed—is sound. We will clarify this in revision.\n\n**On Concurrent Work:**\nWe acknowledge that our treatment of DeepSeek-R1, SCoRe, and DeepSeek-Coder-V2 is brief. SCoRe (Kumar et al. 2024) does not leverage execution feedback at inference, instead asking the model to reconsider its solution—a fundamentally different approach suited for domains without automatic feedback. DeepSeek-R1's application of GRPO to code/math with execution feedback is concurrent and complementary. We did not have quantitative comparisons available at submission time (DeepSeek-R1 was released January 2025, after our work), but we could expand this section to better contextualize relative strengths. Deeper discussion would help position RLEF more clearly.\n\n**Summary:**\nYour review identifies real limitations: domain specificity, potential specialization to the training regime, and incomplete analysis of design choices. We believe these are addressable in revision without new experiments (except for the brief turn limit ablation and hyperparameter sweep, which are reasonable additions). The core contribution—demonstrating that RL training enables LLMs to effectively ground in multi-turn execution feedback—remains sound and well-supported by our analysis."
