      "response": "Thank you for the detailed and constructive review. We appreciate the thorough evaluation and the identification of significant issues that deserve careful responses.\n\n**On the core limitation—underperformance on closely related data:**\n\nYou correctly identify that MixER underperforms task-specific meta-learners on closely related datasets (Section 4.2-4.3). We acknowledge this honestly in the paper's abstract, Section 6 (Limitations), and Discussion. This is not a minor issue but rather reflects a fundamental design tradeoff: MixER sacrifices performance on homogeneous data to enable learning across heterogeneous families. We frame this explicitly in the conclusion: \"MixER excels when processing highly heterogeneous data with limited amounts of training examples, it conversely underperforms classical meta-learning baselines on datasets exhibiting high degrees of relatedness.\"\n\nHowever, we acknowledge that the practical significance is limited if MixER only helps in the narrow regime of loosely related, low-data settings. This is a valid concern about whether the approach actually addresses real scientific discovery needs. We did not propose solving this tradeoff in the current submission, but recognize that either: (a) demonstrating MixER on realistic loosely-related benchmark datasets, or (b) finding architectural or algorithmic modifications that recover performance on related data, would significantly strengthen the work.\n\n**On baseline comparison fairness:**\n\nYou raise a critical point about custom reimplementations. Section C.2 states we perform \"custom reimplementation of the baselines and incorporate them within our MixER layer.\" We want to clarify what this entails: the baseline meta-learners (NCF, CoDA, GEPS) themselves are not modified—only the integration into the MoE framework required architectural changes (e.g., removing softmax aggregation in the MoE layer to allow independent expert operation). \n\nHowever, you are c