from \"switch routing\" (Fedus et al., 2022). We position this as adapting language modeling ideas to scientific computing rather than introducing fundamentally new techniques. We accept that the novelty is primarily in application and problem formulation rather than algorithmic innovation.\n\n**On theoretical justification:**\n\nYou correctly identify that the paper lacks theoretical analysis of: (1) why context-based routing should outperform state-based routing, (2) convergence properties of alternating minimization, and (3) conditions where clustering-based gating beats gradient-based gating. The reference to \"proximal alternating minimization\" and Li et al. (2019) provides general convergence guarantees but not analysis specific to this problem.\n\nWe did not provide this analysis in the submission because: (1) the primary contribution is empirical rather than theoretical, and (2) DSR problem specificity makes general convergence analysis challenging. However, you are correct that additional analysis (even if empirical, e.g., showing why gradient-based gating fails on synthetic hierarchical data) would strengthen the work.\n\n**On the motivating example scaling:**\n\nYou observe that Figure 2 (ODEBench-2, two families) shows dramatic benefits, but scaling to ODEBench-10A/10B shows degradation or inconsistency. This is accurate. On ODEBench-10A (Table 2), MIXER-10 CoDA has adaptation loss 15.9±4.2, which is worse than task-specific baseline. We suspect this degradation occurs because: (1) as the number of families increases, the clustering problem becomes harder (more potential configurations), and (2) with limited environments per family (5 in ODEBench-10A), experts receive insufficient data.\n\nThe paper acknowledges: \"The limited environment count in ODEBench-10A constrains overall performance.\" However, you are correct that this limitation somewhat undermines the narrative of scalability \"to systems of up to ten parametric ordinary differential equations.\" The clear success on two families followed by mixed results on ten families suggests the approach may have limited practical scope.\n\n**On evaluation methodology:**\n\nYou identify inconsistencies: relative L2 (Eq. 9) is used for model selection but standard MSE reported in tables. Hyperparameter choices are not justified (e.g., why 3 experts for classical DSR?). SCCTS and Epilepsy2 use only qualitative clustering visualizations (Figures 6-7) without quantitative metrics.\n\nThese are valid methodological concerns. In the current submission: (1) we justify 3 experts for classical DSR by matching baseline parameter counts, but this is not clearly stated in the main text, and (2) we do not provide quantitative clustering metrics (silhouette scores, NMI, etc.) for time series experiments. These should be added for rigor.\n\n**Summary of our position:**\n\nWe acknowledge that this paper has significant limitations that your review correctly identifies. The core contribution—using K-means and least-squares for context-based MoE routing—is sound but incremental. More critically, the empirical validation is mixed: strong results on loosely related two-family systems but degradation on related data where foundational models are most needed. The baseline comparisons are potentially biased by custom reimplementations, and critical ablations are absent.\n\nThese are not minor issues but rather reflect fundamental questions about whether the approach has sufficient impact and rigor for publication at a top venue. Your review helpfully delineates what would be required to address these concerns: (1) fair baseline comparisons, (2) comprehensive ablations, (3) either stronger empirical results on realistic loosely-related benchmarks or theoretical justification for when the approach helps, and (4) resolution of the performance-relatedness tradeoff."
