inery (Kantorovich ranks from Hallin et al. 2021, entropic maps from Pooladian & Niles-Weed 2021) to conformal prediction. However, we believe the contribution is not merely incremental: (1) **To our knowledge, this is the first application of Kantorovich ranks to conformal prediction**, providing a principled framework for multivariate score ranking; (2) **Lemma 3.3 and Proposition 3.4 provide the first finite-sample coverage guarantee for OT-based multivariate conformal prediction without requiring distributional assumptions or control of approximation error ||Uˆ - U||.** This advances beyond copula-based methods (Messoudi et al. 2021) which may lose coverage when estimated copulas deviate from the truth; (3) **Remark 3.2 shows that OT-CP generalizes Merge-CP** under Gaussian/uniform assumptions, positioning it as a more general framework. In revision, we will emphasize these distinctions more clearly and provide detailed technical comparison with copula-based approaches to better articulate the novelty.\n\n**On Hyperparameter Tuning:**\nThe reviewer correctly identifies that ε and m require careful tuning. Our paper (Section 4.2) notes that both parameters should increase with dimension but provides only heuristic guidance. We agree that practitioners need: (1) principled selection procedures (e.g., validation-based); (2) data-dependent rules relating ε, m to d and n; (3) clear guidance on when each regime is appropriate. The ablation studies in Figure 8 show this trade-off empirically. In revision, we can provide more explicit recommendations, though principled automatic selection remains a challenge for future work.\n\n**On Presentation:**\nWe acknowledge significant clarity issues. The narrative flow in Section 3 moves between discrete transport, continuous formulation, approximations, and implementation without sufficient clarity. The transition from theoretical guarantees (Lemma 3.3) to practical algorithm (Section 3.4) could be clearer. In revision, we will