urements vs. the theoretical O(m^{2h+1.5} log^{h+1} m) bound on a range of graph sizes. These experiments are not part of the current submission but are feasible to implement and will significantly strengthen the evaluation.\n\n**Computational Complexity:**\n\nWe acknowledge that O(m^5.5 log^3 m) for h=n=2 requires empirical validation. We will measure actual runtimes on QM9 and GD graphs and report a detailed timing table showing how polynomial complexity manifests in practice. For large molecules (m ~ 100), this is manageable; for smaller molecules (m ~ 10–50), it is very fast. We will also clarify that for fixed dimension n (e.g., n=3 for molecules), the exponent is a constant, not a variable, which is crucial for practical applicability.\n\n**Key Remaining Limitations:**\n\nThe paper makes strong claims (Problem 1.1) that require rigorous proof. The current submission provides strong intuition and hierarchical definitions but lacks the formal machinery to validate all four conditions (completeness, continuity, invertibility, computability) simultaneously. We believe the proposed revisions (formal reconstruction algorithm, rigorous edge recovery proof, empirical complexity validation, and downstream ML experiments) can address these gaps, but they represent substantial additional work beyond the current submission.\n\nWe view this feedback as constructive guidance toward a more rigorous and empirically grounded contribution, and we are committed to addressing these issues in a revision."
