ests. Our rebuttal proposes multiple seeds, confidence intervals, and t-tests, but the submission contains none of these.\n\n7. **Defense evaluation**: Section 6.6 evaluates only filter-based methods. Our rebuttal proposes adversarial training and certified defenses, but these experiments are not in the paper.\n\n8. **Autocorrelation defense**: Section 7 is one paragraph with no validation. Our rebuttal proposes either removing it or fully implementing it, but neither has been done in the submission.\n\n**Our Response to the Core Concern:**\n\nThe reviewer is right to enforce the principle that papers are evaluated as submitted. However, we respectfully note that the rebuttal phase is designed to address reviewer concerns by clarifying the work or providing additional context, not necessarily to fully resolve every gap identified in a review. Our rebuttal accurately identified the reviewer's concerns and provided substantive, checkable explanations of what would be needed to address them.\n\nThat said, we recognize that many of the identified gaps (especially missing comparisons with Liu et al. 2024b, lack of statistical significance testing, and incomplete ablations) are substantive weaknesses that, if present in a revised submission, would significantly strengthen the paper.\n\n**Path Forward:**\n\nWe are committed to preparing a substantially revised version that addresses the core issues:\n\n1. We will conduct the missing comparison with Liu et al. (2024b) full-series attack, with results clearly presented in the main paper.\n\n2. We will add statistical significance testing with multiple seeds, confidence intervals, and significance annotations to all results.\n\n3. We will add comprehensive ablation studies on key design choices (gradient magnitude handling, convergence criteria, zero-gradient noise parameter Δ).\n\n4. We will either remove the underdeveloped autocorrelation-based defense or fully implement and validate it with experimental results.\n\n5. We w