      "response": "Thank you for the thorough and constructive review. We appreciate the detailed feedback and address each major concern below.\n\n**1. Experimental Scale and Generalization (Major Concern)**\n\nYou correctly identify that our experiments use relatively small models and datasets. We acknowledge this limitation in Appendix A: we train ViTs with embedding dimension 256-384 on CIFAR-10/100 and Tiny ImageNet, and GPT-2 models on Tiny Shakespeare and BookCorpus with only 5 epochs. This reflects resource constraints during development, not a fundamental limitation of the approach.\n\nCrucially, the symmetry framework in Section 3 and Appendix E is *architecture-agnostic* and does not depend on model scale. The key insights—that feed-forward layers exhibit permutation/semi-permutation symmetry, multi-head attention exhibits semi-permutation and invertible symmetry within heads, and residual streams (with RMSNorm) exhibit orthogonal symmetry—apply equally to ViT-Base and ViT-Large as to our smaller models. The alignment algorithms (weight matching, learned matching) are scale-invariant in their formulation, though we recognize that computational complexity may become a practical concern for very large models.\n\nOur results on vision tasks consistently achieve zero or near-zero barriers across three datasets (CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, Tiny ImageNet), and Figure 3c demonstrates width-heterogeneous alignment, suggesting robustness of the approach within the vision domain. We view this as supporting the generality of the symmetry framework, even if not validated on the largest contemporary models. We believe the theoretical contributions and proof-of-concept empirical results merit publication, and we fully agree that validation on standard-scale models (ViT-Base, GPT-2-medium) would substantially strengthen claims about practical applicability.\n\n**2. BookCorpus Discrepancy and Language Model Alignment (Major Concern)**\n\nYou correctly point out that our explanation for the higher BookCorpus barrier (0.42 ± 0.01 vs. ~0 for vision tasks) is speculative and lacks direct evidence. We cited Juneja et al. [2023] to suggest that language models may learn fundamentally different solutions (e.g., lexical vs. syntactic strategies), but we did not verify this in our experiments.\n\nWe emphasize two points: (1) This is an honest observation—the method achieves excellent results on vision but not on this language task, and we report it transparently rather than omitting the result. (2) The barrier is still substantially lower than vanilla averaging (4.34 ± 0.09), and importantly, the Tiny Shakespeare results (0.02 ± 0.00 barrier with learned matching) show that the approach *can* work for language models under different conditions.\n\nThe distinction between Tiny Shakespeare (near-zero barrier) and BookCorpus (0.42 barrier) is telling. Both are language modeling tasks with identical model sizes; the difference may relate to dataset complexity, domain shift between training and test, or the brief 5-epoch training on BookCorpus. We acknowledge this as an open question and agree that rigorous investigation via representation analysis, probing tasks, or loss landscape characterization would be valuable future work. For the current submission, we report the results honestly and note this as a limitation (Appendix A).\n\n**3. Reparameterization Requirements and Practical Implications (Minor Concern)**\n\nYou raise an important point: our framework requires reparameterizations (e.g., RMSNorm reparameterization per Equations 6-8, treating QK/OV circuits as canonical) that alter the network architecture slightly, even while preserving functional equivalence. We acknowledge this in Appendix A.\n\nHowever, we clarify that these reparameterizations are not obstacles in practice:\n\n- **RMSNorm reparameterization (Appendix E):** This is a one-time rewriting of the model for analysis purposes. In practice, we absorb the orthogonal transformation O 