ot compare alternatives in the current submission, the design is well-justified. For future work, comparison with gating-based (Gate = σ(MLP(p)), F_enhanced = Gate ⊙ F_f) or concatenation-based approaches would provide additional insights into mechanism efficiency, though we believe the current approach's simplicity-performance tradeoff is favorable.\n\n**Performance Boundaries and Failure Modes (Minor Concern):**\nThe reviewer requests characterization of behavior outside training ranges and visual failure cases. This is a practical concern for deployment scenarios. Current submission focuses on in-range performance due to space constraints. However, we can provide supplementary analysis: \n\n(1) **Graceful degradation**: Figure 6 demonstrates smooth transitions between degradation levels (levels 3-9), suggesting the model does not exhibit sharp failure modes within reasonable extrapolation ranges.\n\n(2) **Intermediate generalization**: The paper notes training used only anchor points (1, 4, 7, 10) but the model generalizes to levels 3-9, indicating robustness within ±1-2 steps of training bounds.\n\n(3) **Extreme degradations**: While we did not explicitly test noise σ > 20 or blur kernels > 12, real-world AWMM-100k results suggest the model handles authentic complex degradations effectively, likely because real-world degradations are combinations that fall within the learned feature space even if individual parameters exceed training bounds.\n\nFor a revision, we can add supplementary material quantifying performance degradation curves beyond training ranges (σ ∈ [21,30], kernel ∈ [13,20]) and provide visual failure examples if identified. This would strengthen practical applicability guidance.\n\n**Summary:**\nAll three concerns are minor and relate to strengthening presentation and analysis rather than fundamental technical issues. The reviewer's acceptance recommendation reflects recognition that core contributions are sound and the experimental validation is