r p selection:**\nYou correctly point out that the choice of p substantially affects performance, and the paper provides limited principled guidance. This is a fair criticism. We can address this by:\n\n(1) *Proposing a heuristic:* For instance, we could recommend removing the bottom p% of records based on influence scores, with p selected via a small held-out validation set or via sensitivity analysis. We can provide empirical guidance based on the ablations already in Appendix B.6.\n\n(2) *Data-driven approach:* We can analyze the distribution of influence scores and propose removing records below a threshold (e.g., influence < 0th or 10th percentile) rather than a fixed percentage. This would be more adaptive to the specific problem.\n\n(3) *Rules of thumb:* Even a simple practical recommendation (e.g., \"based on our experiments, removing the bottom 20-30% of records by influence is a good starting point; tune p on a small validation set for your specific task\") would significantly improve actionability.\n\n**Summary:**\nYour review identifies important gaps between the conceptual contributions and their empirical/theoretical validation. In a revision, we commit to:\n- Adding ablation studies on π_ref choice\n- Providing quantitative noise analysis for f_return\n- Conducting multi-round validation of f_return's predictive power\n- Empirically comparing SGD and Adam performance\n- Proposing data-driven methods for p selection\n\nThese additions would substantially strengthen the rigor and practical applicability of the work while remaining grounded in experiments we can conduct without fundamental algorithmic changes."
