n's reliability across full training trajectories is not empirically validated in the submitted work.\n   - Important caveat: Figure 6 shows that IIF (which uses f_return to filter iteratively across many rounds) achieves 20-67% sample efficiency gains. This suggests f_return remains meaningful across rounds, but the reviewer is right that direct correlation analysis is missing.\n\n3. **On SGD vs. Adam:**\n   - What is in the paper: Acknowledged limitation in Section 7 that SGD is used as a proxy for Adam.\n   - What is NOT in the paper: No SGD vs. Adam comparison. All experiments use SGD.\n   - The reviewer is correct: this substantially limits practical applicability to modern systems.\n   - Factual note: We mention following Wang et al. [2025b] in using SGD as a proxy, but this does not justify the lack of empirical comparison in our own work.\n\n4. **On hyperparameter p:**\n   - What is in the paper: Figure 6 shows results at different p values (implicitly). Appendix B.6 has ablation results.\n   - What is NOT in the paper: Principled method for selecting p; guidance for practitioners.\n   - The reviewer is correct: practitioners must perform post-hoc tuning; no actionable guidance is provided.\n\n**Our Assessment of the Reviewer's Critique:**\nThe reviewer's evaluation is fair and methodologically sound. They distinguish between:\n- Limitations that are *acknowledged but not addressed* in the submitted paper (SGD, multi-round f_return validation, p selection)\n- Limitations that are *promised in rebuttal but absent from submission* (ablations on π_ref, SGD vs. Adam comparison)\n\nThe reviewer appropriately emphasizes that acceptance should be based on the submitted work, not on promises of future revision. By this standard, the paper has real gaps but remains a solid contribution given its novelty and empirical improvements on the experiments that *are* included.\n\n**What We Can Clarify Without New Experiments:**\n\n1. **On multi-round reliability of f_return: